{"experiments":[  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"For the drug-likeness optimization task (task_id 103) in successful edits, replace operations yield larger improvements in QED_delta than insert operations.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Compare QED_delta distributions for replace vs insert edits within task_id 103 in the successful dataset (train.parquet).",  
      "steps":"1. Load train.parquet.\n2. Filter rows where task_id == 103 and QED_delta is not null.\n3. Subset into two groups by action_type: 'replace' and 'insert'.\n4. Compute summary statistics (mean, median, IQR) of QED_delta in each group.\n5. Perform a statistical test (e.g., Mann–Whitney U or t-test after checking normality) to assess if replace edits have significantly higher QED_delta than insert edits.\n6. Visualize the two distributions via side-by-side boxplots or violin plots.",  
      "deliverables":"1. Summary statistics of QED_delta for replace vs insert in task_id 103.\n2. Test statistic and p-value comparing the two distributions.\n3. Boxplot or violin plot of QED_delta by action_type."  
    }  
  },  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"Larger molecular weight changes (high |MW_delta|) are associated with greater losses in synthetic accessibility (higher SA_delta), and this negative correlation is stronger in unsuccessful edits than in successful edits.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Quantify and compare the correlation between MW_delta and SA_delta in successful vs unsuccessful edits.",  
      "steps":"1. Load train.parquet and train_neg.parquet.\n2. In each dataset, drop rows with null MW_delta or SA_delta.\n3. For each dataset, compute Pearson and Spearman correlation coefficients between MW_delta and SA_delta.\n4. Use Fisher’s Z-transformation to test if the Pearson r for the unsuccessful set is significantly more negative than for the successful set.\n5. Create scatterplots of MW_delta vs SA_delta with regression lines, one for each dataset side by side, and annotate with correlation values.",  
      "deliverables":"1. Table of Pearson and Spearman correlations and sample sizes for each dataset.\n2. Z-test statistic and p-value comparing the two Pearson correlations.\n3. Paired scatterplots with regression lines and annotations."  
    }  
  },  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"In successful edits, replace operations are more likely to preserve the Murcko scaffold compared to insert or delete operations.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Test for an association between action_type and murcko_scaffold_retained in train.parquet.",  
      "steps":"1. Load train.parquet.\n2. Drop rows with null murcko_scaffold_retained.\n3. Build a contingency table of action_type ('replace','insert','delete') vs murcko_scaffold_retained (True/False).\n4. Perform a chi-squared test of independence to assess association.\n5. Visualize the proportions of scaffold retention per action_type via a grouped bar chart.",  
      "deliverables":"1. Contingency table with counts and row/column proportions.\n2. Chi-squared statistic, degrees of freedom, and p-value.\n3. Bar chart showing scaffold retention rates by action_type."  
    }  
  },  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"Unsuccessful edits propose more complex structural changes, as measured by longer 'edit' descriptions (more tokens), compared to successful edits.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Compare the 'edit' description length between train.parquet and train_neg.parquet.",  
      "steps":"1. Load both datasets.\n2. For each row, tokenize the 'edit' string (e.g., split on whitespace and punctuation) and compute an edit_length feature (number of tokens).\n3. Drop rows where edit_length is zero or null.\n4. Compare distributions of edit_length between successful and unsuccessful datasets: compute mean, median, IQR.\n5. Perform a statistical test (Mann–Whitney U) to assess if unsuccessful edits have significantly higher edit_length.\n6. Plot the two distributions via density plots or boxplots.",  
      "deliverables":"1. Summary statistics of edit_length in both datasets.\n2. Test statistic and p-value comparing edit_length distributions.\n3. Density plot or boxplot of edit_length by dataset (successful vs unsuccessful)."  
    }  
  },  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"In successful edits, longer natural-language prompts (more words) correlate with smaller target property improvements (lower target_delta), suggesting that more complex instructions yield diminishing returns.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Assess the relationship between prompt length and target_delta in train.parquet.",  
      "steps":"1. Load train.parquet.\n2. Compute prompt_length as the number of words in the 'prompt' field (split on whitespace).\n3. Drop rows with null target_delta.\n4. Compute Pearson and Spearman correlations between prompt_length and target_delta.\n5. Bin prompt_length into quartiles and compute mean and median target_delta per quartile.\n6. Visualize: scatterplot of prompt_length vs target_delta with regression line, and boxplot of target_delta across prompt_length quartiles.",  
      "deliverables":"1. Correlation coefficients (Pearson, Spearman) and p-values.\n2. Summary table of mean/median target_delta by prompt_length quartile.\n3. Scatterplot with regression and boxplot by quartile."  
    }  
  },  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"Within the permeability optimization task (task_id 105) in successful edits, molecules with moderate MW increases achieve higher target_delta than those with very small or very large MW increases.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Evaluate how MW_delta stratified into quantiles affects target_delta for task_id 105 in train.parquet.",  
      "steps":"1. Load train.parquet.\n2. Filter for task_id == 105 and non-null MW_delta and target_delta.\n3. Divide MW_delta into four quartile bins (Q1–Q4).\n4. For each bin, compute the mean and median target_delta and the count of examples.\n5. Perform one-way ANOVA or Kruskal-Wallis test to check if target_delta differs significantly across MW_delta bins.\n6. Plot MW_delta quartile vs mean target_delta with error bars.",  
      "deliverables":"1. Table of target_delta summary by MW_delta quartile (mean, median, n).\n2. ANOVA or Kruskal-Wallis test statistic and p-value.\n3. Line plot or bar chart of mean target_delta by MW_delta bin."  
    }  
  },  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"In successful edits, replace operations achieve higher QED_delta per unit MW_delta (QED efficiency) than insert or delete operations.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Compare QED_efficiency = QED_delta/MW_delta across action_types in train.parquet.",  
      "steps":"1. Load train.parquet.\n2. Drop rows with null QED_delta or MW_delta, and where MW_delta ≤ 0 to avoid division issues.\n3. Compute QED_efficiency = QED_delta / MW_delta.\n4. Group by action_type and compute summary statistics (mean, median, IQR) of QED_efficiency.\n5. Perform Kruskal–Wallis test to assess differences across action_types.\n6. Visualize via boxplots of QED_efficiency by action_type.",  
      "deliverables":"1. Summary statistics of QED_efficiency for each action_type.\n2. Kruskal–Wallis test statistic and p-value.\n3. Boxplot of QED_efficiency by action_type."  
    }  
  },  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"The probability of a successful edit increases with reductions in synthetic accessibility (negative SA_delta), indicating that making molecules easier to synthesize correlates with overall edit success.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Model the relationship between SA_delta and edit success by pooling successful and unsuccessful datasets.",  
      "steps":"1. Load train.parquet and train_neg.parquet.\n2. Concatenate into a single DataFrame, adding a binary 'success' flag (1 for train.parquet, 0 for train_neg.parquet).\n3. Drop rows with null SA_delta.\n4. Fit a logistic regression model predicting success ~ SA_delta.\n5. Examine the model coefficient for SA_delta, its standard error, and p-value.\n6. Plot predicted success probability vs SA_delta (e.g., using a line plot over SA_delta range).",  
      "deliverables":"1. Logistic regression summary table (coefficients, SEs, p-values).\n2. Plot of predicted success probability as a function of SA_delta.\n3. Discussion of SA_delta’s effect size on success odds."  
    }  
  }  
]}
